<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georges Lemaître</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1894">1894</a>–<a href="/years/1966">1966</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georges Lemaître was a Belgian Catholic priest, physicist and astronomer at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>. In 1927 he proposed that the universe is expanding, deriving the relationship between distance and recession velocity of galaxies later known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubble-s-law">Hubble's law</a>, and in 1931 suggested that it began from a &quot;primeval atom&quot;, the origin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/big-bang">Big Bang</a> theory. He later served as president of the Pontifical Academy of Sciences.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georges-lemaitre.jpg" alt="Georges Lemaître" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GLemaitre30.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Georges Henri Joseph Édouard Lemaître was born on 17 July 1894 in Charleroi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, the eldest of four children of an industrialist who owned a glassworks. He attended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> schools and in 1911 began studying engineering at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-university-of-leuven">Catholic University of Louvain</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he volunteered for the Belgian army, fought in the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yser">Yser</a> and received the Belgian War Cross. Afterwards he turned to physics and mathematics, took his doctorate in 1920 and prepared for the priesthood. He was ordained on 22 September 1923 by Cardinal Mercier, who supported his scientific work.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Lemaître spent 1923 to 1924 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> with Arthur Eddington and the next year at the Harvard College Observatory with Harlow Shapley. In 1927 he published a report arguing, independently of Alexander Friedmann, that the equations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theory-of-relativity">general relativity</a> implied a universe that is not static, and linking this to a proportional relation between the recession velocity of galaxies and their distance. It had little impact because the journal was little read, and Einstein is reported to have said his calculations were correct but his physics abominable. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-hubble">Edwin Hubble</a> published better data in 1929, and in 2018 the International Astronomical Union recommended the name Hubble-Lemaître law.</p><p>In 1931 Lemaître proposed that the universe expanded from a single initial quantum, which he called the primeval atom, now regarded as the first formulation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/big-bang">Big Bang</a> theory. With Manuel Sandoval Vallarta he showed that cosmic rays are charged particles deflected by the Earth's magnetic field. He argued throughout his career for a positive cosmological constant and was an early user of computers in physics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lemaître held that science and religion were not in conflict but should not be mixed. He was professor of physics at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Louvain</a> from 1927 until he retired in 1964, and from 1960 president of the Pontifical Academy of Sciences, a post he held until his death. The Royal Astronomical Society gave him its first Eddington Medal in 1953. In 1965 he learned of the discovery of the cosmic microwave background, which convinced most experts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/big-bang">Big Bang</a>'s validity. He died on 20 June 1966.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Georges Lemaître” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Belgian culture has been prolific, from the painters James Ensor and René Magritte and the Art Nouveau of Victor Horta to the comics of Hergé, while Adolphe Sax invented the saxophone and <b>Georges Lemaître</b> proposed the theory of the expanding universe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.88,4.7&amp;year=1940" aria-label="Leuven on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Georges Lemaître"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5254 5768h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5254 5768h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.88,4.7&amp;year=1940">What was here in 1940? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
